write a personalised 1-week gym plan for a client, based on their onboarding details below. Write it the way a real coach would send it — clear and structured where it needs to be (exercises, sets, reps), but with a personal, conversational voice around it, not like an auto-generated template.

CLIENT DETAILS:
[paste client details here]

HOW TO WRITE IT:

1. Start with a short personal note (2-4 sentences) addressing the client by name — reference their goal and something specific from their answers (schedule, injury history, experience level). Keep it casual, like a coach checking in.

2. Opening mesocycle (month 1, week 1): invent a COMPLETELY UNIQUE muscle-shock split tailored to THIS client's goal, schedule, injuries, training history length, and equipment, not a stock PPL/upper-lower template and never a standard chest/back/arms bro-split. Combine unusual but safe day focuses, exercise order, and density so the first month feels fresh and stimulating. Name each day as "Day N (Weekday): Focus" where Day 1 is Monday, Day 2 Tuesday, … Day 7 Sunday (e.g. "Day 1 (Monday): Lower Power", "Day 2 (Tuesday): Upper Hypertrophy"). Never use bare "Day N" alone or weekday-only headers — the daily tracker needs the weekday in parentheses. Use BASE (lowest) volume for week 1 of the mesocycle and leave 2 to 3 reps in reserve on compounds. Calories should sit at the BASE of the mesocycle band (ready to rise as volume rises in later weeks). Never use hyphen, en dash, or em dash characters in client-facing text.

3. Compound lifts (squat, deadlift, bench, overhead press, rows): 3-6 rep range for strength. Other exercises (isolation/accessory/machine): 8-20 reps, higher reps for smaller muscle groups.

4. Include 3-4 ab/core exercises every training day, varied across the week — don't repeat the same combo daily.

5. Adjust exercises for any limitations the client mentioned (e.g. no pull-ups → lat pulldown, can't squat → leg press/hack squat, no pushups → machine press).

6. Open the plan with a short warmup routine (around 10 minutes) — brisk walk/light cardio, joint mobility, a few bodyweight movements — written as a simple flowing list rather than a rigid numbered table. Mention doing 2 lighter warm-up sets before the first heavy compound lift, phrased naturally (e.g. "before you get into your main lifts, run through a couple of lighter warm-up sets").

7. For each training day, list exercises with sets/reps clearly using "Exercise: N sets x M reps" or "N sets x M to K reps" (plain letter x). Add a short coaching note here and there — a cue, a tip on tempo, what to focus on, written like a coach's aside rather than a formal instruction block.
   CRITICAL: Every training day must list every exercise with sets x reps in full under that day. NEVER write "same as Day 1", "repeat Monday", "follow Thursday's workout", "as above", or any cross-day pointer. If two days use the same session, rewrite the complete exercise list under both day headers. The daily tracker cannot resolve those references.
   Keep each day self-contained. If you add Post-Workout stretches, put them under that same day only. Never put the next day's lifts under Post-Workout / Recovery / Stretching of the previous day. Do not use a lone "Recovery" or "Stretching" line between days.

8. Give a daily step target for every day of the week, including rest/recovery days, varying based on whether it's a training day, active recovery day, or full rest day. Scale steps using the Metabolic Flux Bias section (BUILD-UP / HIGH FLUX raise steps above their current habit; STEADY stays modest). Weave this in naturally rather than as a separate rigid section for every single day.

9. Include one active recovery day and one rest day during the week, with light mobility/stretching suggestions for the recovery day. When flux bias is BUILD-UP or HIGH FLUX, fill allowed session duration with productive density (quality sets within hard day/duration caps) — raise output with the higher food intake, never ego loading past injuries.

10. Mention training intensity guidance in a natural paragraph or two rather than a formal rulebook — leave a rep or two in reserve on compounds, push isolation work closer to failure, and only allow a couple of controlled "cheat reps" on the last set of isolation moves after strict failure (never on squats, deadlifts, RDLs, lunges, or any barbell compound). State this clearly but conversationally.

11. End the week with a short stretching/cooldown routine covering the major muscle groups trained, with rough hold times (30-60 seconds each side), written casually. Place this shared cooldown AFTER all Day N blocks (not between days).

12. Add a short sleep section — duration target (7.5-9 hours), ideal timing based on the client's schedule, and 6-7 practical tips suited to an Indian lifestyle (chai cutoff times, screen habits before bed, meal timing). Write these as friendly suggestions, not a clinical list.

13. Close with a short, encouraging note for the week — no formal "Weekly Focus" bullet section, no "TRAINING INTENSITY RULES" headers, no after-week guidance.

14. Cover all days they can hit the workout. Label each day as "Day N (Weekday)" with Day 1 = Monday through Day 7 = Sunday. Never skip a required training day. Balance upper and lower body across the week — don't overload either.

15. Don't mention the number of week it is, just skip mentioning it

16. **Hard constraints:** Obey the Hard Constraints section — preferred exercises, exercises to avoid, injuries, training days, and gym equipment only.

TONE: Write like an experienced coach messaging a client they train personally — direct and practical for the workout details (sets/reps/exercises can stay in clean list form, that's expected), but wrapped in natural, encouraging language for the intros, transitions, cues, and tips. Avoid robotic section headers like "DAILY STEP TARGET" or "TRAINING INTENSITY RULES" — fold that information into the flow of the plan instead.